-- title:
--   The half-space oblique kernel satisfies the Gaussian kernel hypotheses
-- statement:
--   Let $d\ge0$, $G_s(z)=(\pi s)^{-d/2}e^{-|z|^2/s}$ ($s>0$), $g_a(z)=e^{-a|z|^2}$ with its first, second and third Fréchet derivatives $g^{(1)}_a,g^{(2)}_a,g^{(3)}_a$, $h_N(t)=(2\pi t)^{-d/2}$, the heat kernel $w(t,x,y)=G_{2t}(x-y)$ with its $x$-derivatives $w_1,w_2,w_3$, the constant $A=51\cdot2^{d/2}$, the reflection $R_ex=x-2\langle x,e\rangle e$ and the ray constants $r_K(b)=14+8|b|^2$, $r_C(b,m,\sigma)=r_K(b)^{d/2}e^{m^2/\sigma}\sqrt{\pi r_K(b)\sigma}/2$. For $|e|=1$, $\langle b,e\rangle=0$, let $P_j(b,t,w)$ be the $j$-th $w$-derivative of the directional derivative $P_0(b,t,w)=h_N(t)\,\partial_b g_{1/(2t)}(w)$ of the heat kernel, and the ray kernels $$r_j(t,x,y)=\int_0^\infty P_j\big(b,t,\,x-R_ey+s(e-b)\big)\,ds ,\qquad H_e=\{y:\langle y,e\rangle\ge0\}.$$ For $|e|=1$, $\langle b,e\rangle=0$, the kernel $$K(t,x,y)=w(t,x,y)+w(t,x,R_ey)-2r_0(t,x,y)$$ on $H_e$ (with $x$-derivatives $K_1,K_2,K_3$) satisfies the Gaussian kernel hypotheses of the statement (bounds with constant $o_A(b)$ and spread $r_K(b)$, measurability, local integrable domination, heat equation, unit mass).
--
--   **Formalization Note**: all objects are introduced by `let` in the statement; the statement is the conjunction of the listed facts, each in the exact form used later in the proof.
-- source:
--   A. Friedman, Partial Differential Equations of Parabolic Type, Prentice-Hall, 1964, Chapter 1; D. Gilbarg and N. S. Trudinger, Elliptic Partial Differential Equations of Second Order, Springer, 2001, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-61798-0, Section 6.7, Lemma 6.29 (oblique derivative problem in a half-space); used in Stewart N. Ethier and Thomas G. Kurtz, Markov Processes: Characterization and Convergence, Wiley, 1986, https://doi.org/10.1002/9780470316658, Chapter 8, Section 1

